-- title:
--   Extending a DVR along a simple transcendental extension
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ and $E$ be fields with $E$ an $F$-algebra, and let $x \in E$ be transcendental over $F$ and such that the intermediate field $F(x)$ generated by $\{x\}$ is all of $E$. Let $W$ be a valuation subring of $F$ whose underlying ring is a discrete valuation ring. The assertion is that there exists a valuation subring $V$ of $E$ such that: the underlying ring of $V$ is a discrete valuation ring; every $y \in F$ lying in $W$ has image $\mathrm{algebraMap}\,F\,E\,(y) \in V$; and every $y \in W$ lying in the maximal ideal of the local ring $W$ has image in $E$ belonging to the non-units of $V$, i.e. to the maximal ideal of the valuation subring $V$. Thus $V$ is a discrete valuation subring of $E$ lying over $W$ and dominating it, the domination being expressed by the two displayed conditions on elements rather than by an inclusion of subrings of $F$.
--
--   This is the one-variable transcendental case of the extension of a discrete valuation along a field extension, realised classically by the Gauss valuation $v(\sum a_i x^i) = \min_i v(a_i)$ on $F(x)$. It serves as the transcendental step in the inductive extension of a discrete valuation ring along a finitely generated field extension, and is cited by [`ValuationSubring.exists_isDiscreteValuationRing_dominates_of_adjoin_finset_eq_top`](thm.html#ValuationSubring.exists_isDiscreteValuationRing_dominates_of_adjoin_finset_eq_top).
